3,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-26T21:24:56.957301+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"Kalhana attributes the temple to Lalitaditya Muktapida in the 8th century CE. Later chronicles (Jonaraja, Hasan Ali) say Sikandar Shah Miri destroyed it, though Wikipedia notes scholars caution against taking these accounts at face value; earthquakes caused further damage. It stands today as a ruin, and it is not established whether worship currently takes place there.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Sheikh ul-Alam International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":49.3,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Anantnag railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":10.5,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Martand Sun Temple); Kalhana's Rajatarangini as reported there","architecture_style":"Kashmiri style, blending Gandharan and Gupta influences; a ruined colonnaded courtyard of about 220 ft by 142 ft with a central shrine and 84 smaller shrines. ASI centrally protected","construction_century":8},{"id":"021638d8-38a0-431f-ba9a-b800b2c007d1","name":"Janardanaswamy Temple, Varkala","deity":"Vishnu as Janardanaswamy (Varkaleswara)","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Kerala","district":"Thiruvananthapuram","town":"Varkala","latitude":8.73189,"longitude":76.71,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Parvathy Puthanar River","river_distance_km":3.5,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T09:54:32.209521+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"Located in Janardhanapuram, Varkala, and known locally as Varkaleswara, \"the lord of Varkala\". Wikipedia describes the temple as thousands of years old and renowned within its locality.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Thiruvananthapuram International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":36.1,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Varkala Sivagiri railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":1.7,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Janardanaswamy Temple)","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"022beaed-2b56-49ce-82f0-3cc1178b675c","name":"Chottanikkara Devi Temple","deity":"Bhagavathy (Devi)","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Kerala","district":"Ernakulam","town":"Chottanikkara","latitude":9.9667,"longitude":76.3667,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"pure_veg_available","food_source_name":"Temple Annadanam/Prasadam complex — free meals from 1–3pm daily, plus a special Thiruvonam feast","food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":0.1,"nearest_river_name":"Chitrapuzha River","river_distance_km":0.1,"best_season_notes":"Nov–Jan, around the Makam Thozhal festival.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-03T20:39:31.59741+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"The deity is worshipped in three forms across the day — Saraswati in the morning, Lakshmi at midday, and Durga in the evening. The temple is widely known for rites addressing mental distress, performed at the adjoining Keezhkkavu shrine.","etiquette_notes":"Traditional Kerala dress code: men bare-chested in mundu/dhoti, women in saree or long skirt with blouse. Expect a security check and a no-phones/no-leather rule near the inner shrine, as at most major Kerala temples.","native_name":"ചോറ്റാനിക്കര ദേവീ ക്ഷേത്രം","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Cochin International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":21.2,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Tripunittura railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":2.4,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":null,"architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"029f12eb-b3c6-47d9-a815-27e7c429358c","name":"Galtaji Temple","deity":"Hanuman / Surya / Rama","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Rajasthan","district":"Jaipur","town":"Jaipur","latitude":26.9089,"longitude":75.8611,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Dhund River","river_distance_km":7.1,"best_season_notes":"Oct–Feb, avoiding peak Rajasthan summer heat.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-04T18:27:49.795249+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"Known widely as the \"Monkey Temple\" for the hundreds of macaques living in its Aravalli-hill ravine, alongside seven natural spring-fed water tanks used for ritual bathing. Tradition holds the sage Galav performed 60,000 years of penance here, and that his devotion caused the tanks to be perpetually replenished even in drought. The present temple complex was built under the patronage of Diwan Rao Kripa Ram in the reign of Sawai Jai Singh II.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"गलताजी मंदिर","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Jaipur International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":9.9,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Bais Godam railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":7.1,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Local/regional tradition","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":18},{"id":"0314c2a7-7b0b-419a-be3c-98c8ee452e19","name":"Sabarimala Ayyappa Temple","deity":"Ayyappa","sampradaya":null,"significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Kerala","district":"Pathanamthitta","town":"Sabarimala","latitude":9.4325,"longitude":77.08,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"pure_veg_available","food_source_name":"Annadanam mandapam near the temple — free meals for pilgrims on the trek route","food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":0.5,"nearest_river_name":"Pamba","river_distance_km":4,"best_season_notes":"Open mainly during the Mandalam-Makaravilakku season (mid-Nov to mid-Jan) and monthly first-five-days windows; closed otherwise.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-03T11:51:56.898831+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-04T13:05:55.016033+00:00","sthala_purana":"Sabarimala is held to be the place where Ayyappa, born of Hari and Hara, meditated after subduing Mahishi. Pilgrims observe a forty-one day vratam before the climb and carry the irumudi on the head.","etiquette_notes":"Entry is restricted to those observing the traditional 41-day Ayyappa vratham (austerity vow): black, blue, or saffron dhoti, no leather items carried in, and completion of the vow is expected before the climb. By long-standing custom, women in the traditional \"menstruating age\" range did not enter; India's Supreme Court ruled in 2018 that this restriction is unconstitutional, and the temple's actual practice has varied and remained contested since — check current status before planning a visit if this affects you.","native_name":"ശബരിമല അയ്യപ്പ ക്ഷേത്രം","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":"Devotees observing the traditional 41-day vratham trek roughly 4-5km uphill from Pamba through forest terrain; a shorter alternate path and palanquin services exist for those who need them, though the final stretch involves steps. Not practically wheelchair-accessible.","nearest_airport_name":"Cochin International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":110,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Chengannur Railway Station","nearest_railway_distance_km":90,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":null,"architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"03780324-cf6d-485f-ab18-0b2829a9059d","name":"Mahakuta Group of Temples","deity":"Shiva, across a cluster of shrines","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Karnataka","district":"Bagalkot","town":"Mahakuta","latitude":15.9328,"longitude":75.7217,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Malaprabha River","river_distance_km":3.7,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-30T13:17:24.643882+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A cluster of temples built by the early Badami Chalukya dynasty in the 6th–7th centuries CE, dated by the Mahakuta Pillar inscription (595–602 CE) and a later Vinapoti inscription (696–733 CE). It remains an active Shaiva monastery site and is stylistically linked to the nearby temples of Aihole.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Hubballi Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":93.8,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Lakhmapur railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":12.3,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Mahakuta group of temples)","architecture_style":"Early Chalukya style","construction_century":7},{"id":"037d5f0e-9d88-4bcf-80cd-887352ab7965","name":"Basara Gnana Saraswati Temple","deity":"Saraswati","sampradaya":"Smartha","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Telangana","district":"Nirmal","town":"Basar","latitude":19.3167,"longitude":77.9167,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"pure_veg_available","food_source_name":"Daily Annadanam hall run by the temple, alongside the temple's well-known pulihora and laddu prasadam","food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":0,"nearest_river_name":"Godavari","river_distance_km":0.2,"best_season_notes":"Oct–Mar. Vasant Panchami and children's Aksharabhyasam (first-writing) ceremonies draw the largest crowds.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-03T20:39:31.59741+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:48.914317+00:00","sthala_purana":"One of only two well-known Saraswati temples in India (the other being Kashmir's Sharada Peeth). Tradition holds that sage Vyasa, meditating here after the Mahabharata war, formed three sand hillocks representing Saraswati, Lakshmi, and Kali — the Saraswati mound became this temple.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"బాసర జ్ఞాన సరస్వతి ఆలయం","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Shri Guru Gobind Singh Ji Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":63.8,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Himayatnagar railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":12.2,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Local/regional tradition, not a single classical Sanskrit text","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"03cc11fb-962b-4d4c-90d7-5386e9c0253c","name":"Ambaji Temple","deity":"Amba (Devi)","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":["Shakti Peetha"],"country":"India","state":"Gujarat","district":"Banaskantha","town":"Ambaji","latitude":24.2114,"longitude":72.8508,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"pure_veg_available","food_source_name":"Ambika Bhojanalay (temple-trust-run dining hall)","food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Saraswati (origin, nearby Kumbhariya)","river_distance_km":3,"best_season_notes":"Oct–Feb; Bhadarvi Purnima mela (Aug/Sept) draws huge crowds on foot from Gujarat and Rajasthan.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-03T11:51:56.898831+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-22T19:09:34.948917+00:00","sthala_purana":"One of the Shakti Peethas, traditionally held to mark where Sati's heart fell. Ambaji is the kuldevi (family deity) of many Gujarati and Rajasthani communities, and uniquely has no idol in the main shrine — worship is offered instead to a visible yantra (a geometric diagram) set into the wall, considered too sacred to be looked upon directly by some accounts.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"अंबाजी मन्दिर","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":"Per the temple trust's own site: morning aarti 6–6:30am, morning darshan 6:30–11:30am, afternoon darshan 12:30–5pm, evening aarti 7–7:30pm, evening darshan 7:30pm–midnight. Some tourism aggregators instead list a shorter evening window closing around 9pm — the temple trust's own figures are used here.","accessibility_notes":"Wheelchairs are available at the VIP Plaza (Main Shakti Gate), with an assistance point for senior citizens there too; darshan queue wait is typically 30–40 minutes. This is distinct from the nearby Gabbar Hill shrine (a related but separate pilgrimage point), which involves 999+ steps and is not wheelchair-accessible.","nearest_airport_name":"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel International Airport, Ahmedabad","nearest_airport_distance_km":185,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Abu Road Railway Station","nearest_railway_distance_km":20,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Devi Bhagavata Purana / Shakti Peetha tradition (Sati-limb legend)","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"04b6e31d-c71b-427b-b950-f69cf0e73031","name":"Gayatri Gyan Mandir, Mahuda","deity":"Gayatri","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Jharkhand","district":"Dhanbad","town":"Singra","latitude":23.74867,"longitude":86.26389,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Katri River","river_distance_km":3.1,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:38:38.196668+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A temple in Washery Colony near Mahuda Junction railway station, Dhanbad district, Jharkhand, where the goddess Gayatri is worshipped. The campus is near Mahuda Bazaar, and the Mahadeo Mandir is the closest other temple.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Dhanbad Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":19,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Mahuda railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":0.6,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Gayatri Gyan Mandir, Mahuda), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"04ff7e1b-0faa-4aee-b7c0-50a3e72cd324","name":"Ahobilam Narasimha Temple","deity":"Narasimha","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Andhra Pradesh","district":"Nandyal","town":"Ahobilam","latitude":15.1333,"longitude":78.7167,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"pure_veg_available","food_source_name":"Sri Ahobila Matam's Annamacharya Nitya Annadanam Trust — free curd rice morning and evening, full meals (Tadiyaradhanam) at noon","food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":0,"nearest_river_name":"Bhavanasini","river_distance_km":0.5,"best_season_notes":"Oct–Feb. The nine shrines are spread across forested hills; the upper ones need a hike.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-03T13:44:40.752775+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:48.914317+00:00","sthala_purana":"The kshetra of the nine forms of Narasimha (Nava Narasimha), held to be the place where Vishnu took the man-lion avatara and slew Hiranyakashipu. It is also the seat of the Ahobila Matha of the Sri Vaishnava sampradaya.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"అహోబిలం నరసింహ స్వామి ఆలయం","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":"Ahobilam's Lower Shrine (Diguva Ahobilam) is easily accessible; the Upper Shrine (Eguva Ahobilam) with the original cave temple requires a genuine forest hike and is not wheelchair-accessible.","nearest_airport_name":"Kadapa Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":68.9,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Diguvametta railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":31.4,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":null,"architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"051b65f5-254b-4fea-b60a-baa8a6e91c1d","name":"Umananda Temple, Guwahati","deity":"Shiva (Bhasmachala/Umananda)","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Assam","district":"Kamrup Metropolitan","town":"Guwahati","latitude":26.1889,"longitude":91.7453,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Brahmaputra","river_distance_km":0,"best_season_notes":"Oct–Feb; the temple is on an island reached by ferry from the Guwahati bank.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-04T13:57:28.972216+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:48.914317+00:00","sthala_purana":"Built in 1694 under the Ahom king Gadadhar Singha on what is often called the world's smallest inhabited river island, in the middle of the Brahmaputra — largely destroyed in the 1897 Assam earthquake and later rebuilt by a local merchant. The site is also called Bhasmachala, \"hill of ashes,\" from a tradition in the Kalika Purana that Shiva burned Kamadeva to ash here with his third eye after being disturbed mid-meditation; the temple's name, Umananda, refers to the joy (ananda) Shiva is said to have created the island to give his wife Uma.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"ঊমানন্দ মন্দিৰ","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Lokpriya Gopinath Bordoloi International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":18.1,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Guwahati railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":0.9,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Local/regional tradition, referencing the Kalika Purana","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":17},{"id":"056e56c2-6389-458a-b4d5-c4a8c3257692","name":"Sree Poornathrayeesa Temple","deity":"Vishnu as Santhanagopala Murthy (Poornathrayeesa)","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Kerala","district":"Ernakulam","town":"Thrippunithura","latitude":9.94503,"longitude":76.34222,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Chathari River","river_distance_km":1.6,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T10:59:23.880063+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A Vishnu temple in Thrippunithura, Kochi, believed to bless childless couples with children. It served as the national deity's seat of the former Kingdom of Cochin and was the first of its eight royal temples. A fire in 1920 led to the present concrete structure. Its eight-day Vrischikotsavam is known for elephant processions.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Cochin International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":24.2,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Tripunittura railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":1.3,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Sree Poornathrayeesa Temple), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":"Traditional Kerala temple architecture","construction_century":null},{"id":"056f8685-125a-4649-9152-b6fd64afdb2f","name":"Keshav Rai Temple","deity":"Vishnu as Keshav Rai","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Rajasthan","district":"Bundi","town":"Patolia","latitude":25.289,"longitude":75.939,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Chambal River","river_distance_km":null,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:38:01.041713+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:48.914317+00:00","sthala_purana":"A temple to Keshav Rai, a name of Vishnu, on the bank of the Chambal River at Keshoraipatan, Rajasthan. It was built by Rao Raja Chhatrasal in 1641.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Kota Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":17.1,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Keshorai Patan railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":3.8,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Keshav Rai Temple), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":17},{"id":"0599b499-8c48-41b5-bf28-2f7c1ae0950b","name":"Mindhal Mata Temple","deity":"Mindhal Mata (Chamunda)","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Himachal Pradesh","district":"Chamba","town":"Sach","latitude":32.98333,"longitude":76.45,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Chenab","river_distance_km":null,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:04:47.875457+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:48.914317+00:00","sthala_purana":"A temple in the Pangi Valley of Chamba district, Himachal Pradesh, on the left bank of the Chenab opposite Sach, also called the temple of Chamunda. It has long been a pilgrimage place, and a fair is held in the month of Bhadon attended by people from nearby valleys. The priest and chela are Brahmans.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Kangra Airport, Gaggal","nearest_airport_distance_km":92.7,"nearest_railway_station_name":null,"nearest_railway_distance_km":null,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Mindhal Mata Temple), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"06b7c2e6-10f1-46f9-86b6-ed31dea8a7d2","name":"Virupaksha Temple, Hampi","deity":"Shiva (Virupaksha)","sampradaya":null,"significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Karnataka","district":"Ballari","town":"Hampi","latitude":15.335,"longitude":76.46,"sandhya_friendly":"yes","sandhya_notes":"The Tungabhadra river runs past the Hampi ruins, a short walk from the temple.","samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Tungabhadra","river_distance_km":1,"best_season_notes":"Oct–Feb; one of the few continuously active temples within the Hampi UNESCO site.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-03T11:51:56.898831+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-05T18:23:31.36475+00:00","sthala_purana":"Among the oldest continuously worshipped temples in India, with inscriptions and shrine elements predating the Vijayanagara empire that later grew up around it. Tradition holds that the local goddess Pampa (for whom the Tung