na_source":"Wikipedia (Kamleshwar Mahadev Temple), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"1178e539-b809-4a34-a857-bbdcd9a45a24","name":"Shore Temple, Mahabalipuram","deity":"Shiva (two shrines, facing east and west) with a reclining Vishnu shrine between them","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":["Pallava monument"],"country":"India","state":"Tamil Nadu","district":"Chengalpattu","town":"Mahabalipuram","latitude":12.6161,"longitude":80.1985,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":null,"river_distance_km":null,"best_season_notes":"Nov–Feb.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-08T06:23:03.123234+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:48.914317+00:00","sthala_purana":"Built by the Pallava king Narasimhavarman II (Rajasimha) in the early 8th century on the Coromandel coast, this granite structural temple is the oldest of its kind in South India and marks the transition from the rock-cut mandapams and rathas carved earlier at the same site to free-standing built temples. Local tradition holds it is the last-surviving of an original group of Seven Pagodas, the other six long since swallowed by the sea. It forms part of the UNESCO World Heritage Site \"Group of Monuments at Mahabalipuram\", inscribed in 1984.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"கடற்கரைக் கோயில்","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Chennai International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":42.1,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Otivakkam railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":24.2,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"UNESCO World Heritage documentation; ASI records; local tradition","architecture_style":"Pallava","construction_century":8},{"id":"121fa3e9-440b-4f35-9aa8-1498a7677670","name":"Paschimesvara Siva Temple","deity":"Shiva (lingam)","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Odisha","district":"Khordha","town":"Bhubaneswar Municipal Corporation","latitude":20.24611,"longitude":85.83389,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Dayaa River","river_distance_km":3.8,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:38:38.196668+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A ruined Shiva temple in Bhubaneswar dating to the 8th century, in the Bhaumakara period. The superstructure fell long ago, though the linga is still visible among fallen stones. Detached sculptures of Kartikeya, Ganesha, Parvati and Narasimha help date it, and steps from the west suggest it once faced west.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Biju Patnaik International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":2.3,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Bhubaneswar railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":2.5,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Paschimesvara Siva Temple), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":8},{"id":"124d71f2-04e3-4de3-8617-69c877aa9d60","name":"Shree Siddhi Vinayak Ganesh Temple, Nagda","deity":"Ganesha as Siddhi Vinayak","sampradaya":"Ganapatya","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Madhya Pradesh","district":"Dewas","town":"N