note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-26T21:24:56.957301+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"Kalhana's Rajatarangini names the hill Gopadri and has King Gopaditya build a Jyeshtheshvara shrine there, which Wikipedia dates to about 371 BCE; tradition also credits Jaloka, a son of Ashoka. Dating of the standing structure is disputed: Fergusson placed it in the 17th–18th century, while Aurel Stein held that the superstructure is recent but the base and stairs are much older, and there is no scholarly consensus. Kashmiri Hindu tradition links the hill to Adi Shankara and to the composition of the Saundarya Lahari. Gulab Singh built steps from the Durga Naag side, and the temple has been managed by the Dharmarth Trust since the 19th century.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":"About 240 steps lead to the sanctum. A 5.6 km road built in 1969 serves the hill, but part of it is closed to the public. Wheelchair access is not verified.","nearest_airport_name":"Sheikh ul-Alam International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":11.3,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Srinagar railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":6.1,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Shankaracharya Temple); Kalhana's Rajatarangini as reported there","architecture_style":"Octagonal base of about 20 ft with a square building above and a circular inner chamber; an ASI-protected monument","construction_century":null},{"id":"1cd8da7c-dcfd-4be8-93d5-c727ba1fddfc","name":"Karpaka Vinayakar Temple","deity":"Ganesha as Karpaka Vinayakar","sampradaya":"Ganapatya","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Tamil Nadu","district":"Sivagangai","town":"Pillayarpatti","latitude":10.1193,"longitude":78.6678,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Palar River","river_distance_km":10.4,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T10:59:23.880063+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A rock-cut cave shrine at Pillayarpatti in Sivaganga district, Tamil Nadu, dated to the 7th century and expanded over later centuries. The cave holds rock-cut images of Ganesha and a Shiva linga with unusual iconography. Inscriptions help date its core, and it is one of the nine ancestral temples of the Nagarathars.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Madurai International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":70.7,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Chettinad railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":12.7,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Karpaka Vinayakar Temple), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":"Rock-cut cave shrine","construction_century":7},{"id":"1d048cce-9686-42b0-b3ce-b4f39654cdce","name":"Khandoba Temple, Jejuri","deity":"Khandoba / Malhari Martand (Shiva)","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Maharashtra","district":"Pune","town":"Jejuri","latitude":18.2833,"longitude":74.1667,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Karha River","river_distance_km":null,"best_season_notes":"Nov–Dec for Champa Shashti, Khandoba's principal festival; otherwise Oct–Feb.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-04T13:20:34.20482+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-30T13:12:14.428399+00:00","sthala_purana":"Khandoba, a form of Shiva/Martanda-Bhairava, is worshipped here as the patron deity of the Marathas; the temple became a symbol of Maratha military pride, and houses Portuguese church bells brought as war trophies by Chimaji Appa after the 1737 Battle of Vasai.","etiquette_notes":"At the Bhandara festival, devotees throw turmeric powder (bhandara) over each other and the temple — a festival custom rather than a standing dress code.","native_name":"श्री खंडोबा मंदिर, जेजुरी","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":"No published official schedule was found.","accessibility_notes":"Nearly 200 steps lead to the main sanctum, approachable by three flights of steps (east, west and north, the north being the main entrance); not documented as wheelchair accessible.","nearest_airport_name":"Pune International Airport (Lohegaon)","nearest_airport_distance_km":55,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Jejuri railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":4,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Khandoba Temple, Jejuri); Deccan Herald","architecture_style":"Hemadpanthi","construction_century":12},{"id":"1d9466ff-2760-4b88-91d2-ccc2886d475c","name":"Valeeswarar Temple, Mylapore","deity":"Valeeswarar (Shiva) / Periyanayagi Amman","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":["Saptha Sthana Mylapore"],"country":"India","state":"Tamil Nadu","district":"Chennai","town":"Mylapore, Chennai","latitude":13.0345,"longitude":80.2685,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Adyar River","river_distance_km":2.1,"best_season_notes":"Oct–Feb.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moder