urce":"Contributor-submitted local survey; local/regional tradition","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"222ac511-ea7a-406b-bfd1-0ff1a74995dc","name":"Amareswara Temple, Amaravati","deity":"Amareswara (Shiva)","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":["Pancharama Kshetra"],"country":"India","state":"Andhra Pradesh","district":"Palnadu","town":"Amaravati","latitude":16.573,"longitude":80.358,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Krishna","river_distance_km":0.1,"best_season_notes":"Feb–Mar for Maha Shivaratri; otherwise Oct–Feb.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-04T17:30:52.99453+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:48.914317+00:00","sthala_purana":"Another of the five Pancharama Kshetras formed, by tradition, from the shattered fragments of Shiva's throat-linga after Tarakasura's slaying — this one held to have been installed by Indra himself. Unique among the Pancharamas for standing on the Krishna river rather than near the Godavari. The temple's earliest inscription is dated 1129 CE, and it was substantially expanded in the 18th–19th centuries under the Chintapalli/Dharanikota ruler Vasireddy Venkatadri Naidu; its sanctum holds a 15-foot white marble linga.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"అమరావతి అమరేశ్వర ఆలయం","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Vijayawada Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":47.8,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Pedakurapadu railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":16.8,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Local/regional tradition","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":12},{"id":"229bdcf9-b0bd-4d51-9732-456dbf4957b2","name":"Thiruvazhmarban Temple, Thiruppathisaram","deity":"Thiruvazhmarban / Kuralappa Perumal (Vishnu)","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":["Malai Nadu Divya Desam"],"country":"India","state":"Tamil Nadu","district":"Kanyakumari","town":"Thiruppathisaram","latitude":8.2086,"longitude":77.4472,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Pazhayar River","river_distance_km":0.4,"best_season_notes":"Oct–Feb.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-06T08:12:33.253723+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"One of the Malai Nadu Divya Desams, also known as Thiruvanparisaram, traditionally held to have been built by the Chera king Kulasekara. The nine-foot image of Kuralappa Perumal is said to be made of kadu sarkara yogam, a traditional lime-based mortar. The oldest surviving inscription dates to 1139 CE, and the village is also revered as the birthplace of the Alvar saint Nammalvar's mother.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"திருவாழ்மார்பன் கோயில்","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Thiruvananthapuram International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":65.5,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Nagercoil Junction railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":3.8,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Local/regional tradition; temple inscriptions","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":12},{"id":"22ef031e-c534-4dd1-b208-723d9b49551c","name":"Tripura Sundari Temple, Udaipur","deity":"Tripura Sundari (Devi)","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":["Shakti Peetha"],"country":"India","state":"Tripura","district":"Gomati","town":"Udaipur","latitude":23.5167,"longitude":91.4667,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Gomati","river_distance_km":2,"best_season_notes":"Oct–Mar; Diwali-time Tripura Sundari mela is the major local festival.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-03T11:51:56.898831+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:48.914317+00:00","sthala_purana":"One of the Shakti Peethas, traditionally held to mark where the right foot of Sati fell. Tripura Sundari has long been the principal state deity of the former kingdom of Tripura, and the temple's distinctive hut-shaped (kurma, or tortoise-back) roof, unusual among Shakti Peethas, is said to have been modelled on a local Bengali temple style by the Tripura royal family who built it in the 15th century.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"ত্রিপুরা সুন্দরী মন্দির","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Maharaja Bir Bikram Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":47.4,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Udaipur railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":1.5,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Devi Bhagavata Purana / Shakti Peetha tradition (Sati-limb legend)","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"23876bcf-5093-45d5-870a-2fb2d0e4c5be","name":"Madhyamaheshwar Temple","deity":"Shiva (Madhyamaheshwar)","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":["Panch Kedar"],"country":"India","state":"Uttarakhand","district":"Rudraprayag","town":"Gaundar","latitude":30.63694,"longitude":79.21611,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Markanda Ganga River","river_distance_km":1.7,"best_season_notes":"Open roughly May–Oct; in winter the deity is carried down to Omkareshwar Temple in Ukhimath, about 36 km away, where it is worshipped for six months.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-05T08:35:20.91066+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"The second of the Panch Kedar temples, set in a high meadow, where tradition holds the navel (nabhi) of Shiva's bull-form surfaced after the Pandavas pursued him for absolution following the Mahabharata war. A black-stone, navel-shaped Shivalinga is enshrined in the sanctum in his memory.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"मध्यमहेश्वर मंदिर","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Dehradun Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":110.9,"nearest_railway_station_name":null,"nearest_railway_distance_km":null,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Local/regional tradition","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"23b592b1-e8ad-4b28-9263-82623c076ba5","name":"Gangotri Temple","deity":"Ganga (Devi)","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":["Chota Char Dham"],"country":"India","state":"Uttarakhand","district":"Uttarkashi","town":"Gangotri","latitude":30.9993,"longitude":78.9411,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Bhagirathi River","river_distance_km":0.6,"best_season_notes":"May–Oct only — the temple closes for winter and the deity is moved to Mukhba village; Kedarnath and Badrinath close on a similar schedule.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-04T17:30:52.99453+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"One of the Chota Char Dham of Uttarakhand (alongside Yamunotri, Kedarnath and Badrinath), built by the Gurkha general Amar Singh Thapa in the early 19th century and later restored by Jaipur's Maharaja Madho Singh II. Tradition holds King Bhagiratha's long penance brought the Ganga down from the heavens, and that Shiva agreed to break her fall through his matted hair so the force wouldn't devastate the earth — a separate tradition holds Rama and Hanuman both visited and worshipped Ganga here.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"गंगोत्री मन्दिर","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Dehradun Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":115.5,"nearest_railway_station_name":null,"nearest_railway_distance_km":null,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Local/regional tradition","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null}]$rtam$::jsonb)
